INT. DUMPLING KITCHEN - BACK TABLE - NIGHT

Steam curls from bamboo baskets. A wall of orange tiles, a radio murmuring in Cantonese. Four people crowd a stainless counter, pleating dumplings by hand.

CELESTE MARCHAND, 30s, silk blouse, sleeves rolled, pleats each one with great concentration. RORY BANERJEE, 30s, sits across from her, flour on his glasses. NONNA LUCIA, 80s, wiry, in a faded apron, oversees them with a wooden spoon. TOMASZ WISNIEWSKI, 50s, the chef, passes behind with a tray, wiping his hands on his hip.

NONNA LUCIA
(to the table)
Too much filling. You will burst them.

CELESTE
I like them fat.

RORY
(softly)
She does.

Celeste's hands pause. She doesn't look up. He reaches for a wrapper, their fingers brush the flour, and neither of them moves for a beat.

TOMASZ
Everyone eat! Plates are coming!

NONNA LUCIA
(to Rory)
You. You are the one from the letters.

RORY
Which letters?

NONNA LUCIA
The ones she does not send.

Celeste drops a dumpling. It lands on the tile with a wet slap. Tomasz glides by and stoops to retrieve it, humming.

CELESTE
(to herself)
Okay.

RORY
Celeste--

CELESTE
It's fine. Tell Tomasz the soy is low.

She stands, wipes her hands, and walks out through the swinging door. Rory watches it swing. Nonna Lucia pats the empty chair.

NONNA LUCIA
Sit. Pleat. It is always better in the second hour.
